;; THE SAME QUESTIONS ASKED OF EVERY TARGET'S ARTIFACT, one expected answer each.
;;
;; `DECISIONS.md#four-operations` says the SEMANTICS are identical on every target
;; and only the spelling is not. This file is that sentence as data, so a second
;; target cannot quietly answer a different question or a different value for the
;; same program. `bin/check-four-ops` reads it; each target's harness answers it.
;; Nothing here is JVM-specific.
;;
;; WHY A FILE AND NOT FOUR HARNESSES. The failure this guards against is not a
;; target answering wrongly -- that shows up immediately -- it is two targets
;; answering DIFFERENT QUESTIONS and both passing. A metadata section called
;; `com.3sln.flint.meta` on one and `flint` on the other is a contract with two
;; spellings, and the only way a check sees it is by being handed the name once.
;;
;; IT IS `contract.edn` AND WAS `prop.edn`. `prop` was the fourth operation and is
;; gone: the metadata is what the container carries, not what a call answers.

{;; --------------------------------------------------------------- the program
 ;;
 ;; The smallest thing that still pulls in `clojure.core`, which is what makes the
 ;; native count interesting: 88 is `clojure.core`'s reach and not this program's.
 :program "(ns t)\n(defn main [args] \"x\")\n"
 :entry "t/main"
 :call-args ["hello"]
 :expect-answer "x"

 ;; --------------------------------------------------------- the three operations
 ;;
 ;; THREE, not four. A harness reporting a fourth is reporting an operation this
 ;; contract does not have, and the checker says so -- the drift to guard against is
 ;; a target keeping `prop` because its host found it convenient.
 :operations ["boot" "loop" "link"]

 ;; --------------------------------------------------------------- the metadata
 ;;
 ;; IN THE CONTAINER, read WITHOUT executing the artifact. Every format has a path
 ;; the platform already understands -- a wasm custom section, a JVM class
 ;; attribute, a CLR CustomAttribute row -- and all three are FLAT namespaces
 ;; shared with everything else in the toolchain. JVMS 4.7 requires an unrecognised
 ;; class attribute to be silently ignored, so on the JVM a collision is QUIET: the
 ;; wrong data is read as the right data and nothing complains.
 :meta
 {:section "com.3sln.flint.meta"
  :must-not-answer-to "flint"
  :why-namespaced "It was the bare word `flint` until 2026-09-24. `flint.modmeta/section-name`
                   is the one place it is written for the compiler; this file is the
                   one place it is written for the checker, and the check is that they
                   agree."
  ;; Keys the metadata must carry, whatever else it carries. Read with a real EDN
  ;; reader, because the container hands over text and the checker has one -- which
  ;; is the argument that killed `prop`: a runtime answering `version` from inside
  ;; needed ~100 lines of delimiter-counting to find it.
  :keys [:flint/module :version :compat :exports]
  ;; AND `:exports` MUST BE THE OPERATIONS, exactly. It said `["boot" "link" "loop"
  ;; "prop"]` for a while after `prop` was removed from the class -- the artifact had
  ;; three operations and its own metadata advertised four, which is worse than either
  ;; being wrong alone, because a host reading the metadata would look up a method
  ;; that is not there. Checking that the two AGREE is what finds it; checking only
  ;; that `:exports` is present does not.
  :exports-are-the-operations true
  ;; `:version` comes from `meta.edn`, which `bin/check-version` already gates
  ;; across every manifest. This gates that it reaches the artifact too.
  :version-from "meta.edn"
  ;; PER-TARGET on purpose, so no literal here: a wasm module's compatibility key
  ;; covers a linear-memory ABI the JVM and the CLR have not got, so the keys MUST
  ;; differ and a shared value would be asserting a collision. What is shared is the
  ;; SHAPE, and that two builds of one program agree.
  :compat-key-matches "^[0-9a-f]{8}$"}

 ;; --------------------------------------------------------------- loop
 ;;
 ;; THE EXISTING ABI NUMBERS, which this project treats as the ABI itself.
 :status
 {0 "Done"
  1 "Threw"
  2 "NeedsHost -- THE RESTING STATE. The control plane is a green thread parked on
     the system port, so a healthy idle sandbox reports it, and a harness that
     treats 2 as failure fails on every working program."
  3 "Shelved -- the sandbox exported itself and stopped. ALREADY RETURNED by the
     runtime before it was written down anywhere: `runtime/src/snap.rs` sets it and
     no face declared it, so both contracts and all four faces agreed on three
     numbers while the runtime had four."
  4 "Wedged -- nothing runnable and nothing a host can supply, because the threads
     that remain are waiting on each other. NOT `Done`, which is what the scheduler
     answered until 2026-09-25.

     REACHABLE ONLY WITHOUT A SYSTEM PORT, and no current entry point is: starting
     a program goes through the control plane, which parks on that port and makes
     `sched-needs-host` true, so a sandbox with a door answers 2 and lets the host
     decide it has nothing left to send. `kin/sched.drivers` case 10 exercises the
     branch directly on all three runtimes; nothing exercises it end to end, and
     that is a property of how a program is started rather than of this number."}
 ;; What a sandbox answers with nothing queued, and again after a call has been
 ;; served. Both 2: serving a call does not finish the program.
 :loop {:at-rest 2 :after-call 2}

 ;; --------------------------------------------------------------- link
 :link
 {:calls-in-normal-case 0
  :before-boot :accepted
  :after-boot :refused
  :why "Natives resolve exactly once when the image loads, so an override
        registered after boot would silently never apply. Refusing is the contract:
        a host must not be left holding a hook it believes in. And the normal case
        is ZERO calls -- a bulk or resolver API requiring all 88 would reject
        programs that run."}

 ;; --------------------------------------------------------------- boot
 :boot
 {:declared-natives 88
  :on-missing-native :fail
  :message-names-it true
  :why "SHARED ACROSS TARGETS, and the strongest number in the file: the natives a
        program declares are a property of the IMAGE, so every target compiling
        this program must find the same 88. A target seeing 87 or 89 has a
        different image or a different clojure.core.

        Failing on a missing one is a deliberate change away from the null-tolerance
        in `runtime/src/image.rs` and `Img.java`: the VERSION says what has to be
        linked, so a gap is a version mismatch. A version-matched host carries all
        88 and nothing changes; a deliberately trimmed runtime now fails to boot a
        program it could have run, and that trade is chosen."}}
